# 📶 LeetCode 75 — Day 39: Maximum Level Sum of a Binary Tree

**📌 Problem:** [1161. Maximum Level Sum of a Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-level-sum-of-a-binary-tree/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Tree, Binary Tree, BFS
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

Since the problem asks for the level whose values add up to the most, and BFS naturally processes a tree one full level at a time, this is really just "run BFS, total each level's values as you go, and remember which level had the biggest total."

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Initialize `maxSum = INT_MIN` (so even a single negative-value tree is handled correctly) and `resultlevel = 0`.
2. Start BFS with a queue containing just `root`, and track `currlevel = 1`.
3. For each level:
   - Record `n = que.size()` — the number of nodes in this level.
   - Process exactly `n` nodes: pop each one, add its value to a running `sum`, and push its children (if any) for the next level.
   - After processing the whole level, compare `sum` to `maxSum`. If it's bigger, update `maxSum = sum` and `resultlevel = currlevel`.
   - Increment `currlevel` before moving to the next level.
4. Return `resultlevel` once the whole tree has been traversed — this is the level (1-indexed) with the maximum sum.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n)` — every node is visited and processed exactly once.
- **Space complexity:** `O(n)` — the queue can hold up to the width of the widest level in the worst case.

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method), using level-order BFS with a running per-level sum.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxLevelSum(TreeNode* root) {
        int maxSum = INT_MIN, resultlevel = 0;
        
        queue<TreeNode*> que;
        que.push(root);
        int currlevel = 1;

        while (!que.empty()) {
            int n = que.size();
            int sum = 0;

            while (n--) {
                TreeNode* temp = que.front();
                que.pop();

                sum += temp->val;

                if (temp->left) {
                    que.push(temp->left);
                }

                if (temp->right) {
                    que.push(temp->right);
                }
            }
            if (sum > maxSum) {
                maxSum = sum;
                resultlevel = currlevel;
            }
            currlevel++;
        }
        return resultlevel;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

Starting `maxSum` at `INT_MIN` (rather than `0`) matters here — trees can have negative values, so a level sum could legitimately be negative and still be the "maximum." Initializing to `0` would silently produce a wrong answer for such cases without ever throwing an error.

